[s3;:1:%- 1. Sculptor`'s Architecture for Interactive Applications&]
[s5;%- Building a real`-time simulation requires managing the software 
renderer`'s state alongside an active physics environment.&]
[s22;:1`.1:%- 1.1. Separation of Simulation and Rendering&]
[s5;%- In an interactive application, relying on the screen`'s refresh 
rate to drive game logic causes inconsistent physics behavior 
across different hardware. The architecture must decouple the 
visual pipeline from the mathematical simulation. Therefore [^topic`:`/`/Sculptor`/src`/Upp`_Sculptor`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:Sculptor^ S
culptor] rendering engine simply draws the current state of the 
world, while a dedicated update loop calculates velocities, gravity, 
and collisions using a fixed time delta.&]
[s22;:1`.2:%- 1.2. Hierarchical Matrix Scoping&]
[s5;%- When objects exist within local coordinate spaces (such as 
a rolling ball sitting on top of a tilting board) transformations 
must be applied hierarchically. The [^topic`:`/`/Sculptor`/src`/Upp`_Sculptor`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:Sculptor^ S
culptor] engine prefers RAII`-based matrix scoping using [^topic`:`/`/Sculptor`/src`/Upp`_Sculptor`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:Sculptor`:`:Scope^ S
culptor`::Scope] objects. Pushing a matrix scope allows global 
transformations (like the board`'s tilt) to apply universally, 
while nested scopes allow individual objects (like the rotating 
ball) to undergo local translation and rotation without corrupting 
the global matrix stack for subsequent draw calls.&]
[s22;:1`.3:~~~480;%- 1.3. Camera Math and Spatial Navigation&]
[s5;%- A dynamic orbit camera calculates its position in 3D space 
using spherical coordinates. By maintaining a target focal point, 
a distance radius, a yaw angle, and a pitch angle, the camera 
coordinates are derived using trigonometric functions. The renderer`'s 
view matrix is then aligned by directing the calculated camera 
position toward the focal origin.&]
[s3;:2: 2. Core Concepts and Usage Principles&]
[s5; A 3D labyrinth simulation relies on three fundamental systems 
operating in tandem.&]
[s22;:2`.1: 2.1. Geometric Physics and Collision Resolution&]
[s5; Smooth physical interactions require iterative collision resolution. 
When a moving sphere intersects with a static bounding box (a 
wall), the physics engine calculates the squared distance between 
the sphere`'s center and the closest clamped point on the box`'s 
perimeter. If the distance is less than the sphere`'s radius, 
an intersection has occurred. The physics solver then ejects 
the sphere along the collision normal and dampens its velocity. 
Running this solver iteratively ensures the sphere slides smoothly 
along flat surfaces and prevents it from passing through sharp 
corners.&]
[s22;:2`.2: 2.2. Procedural Spatial Layouts&]
[s5; Instead of hardcoding complex 3D meshes, grid`-based environments 
like labyrinths are best generated procedurally from 2D data 
arrays. By parsing a localized grid map composed of string characters, 
the system dynamically calculates the 3D coordinates for walls, 
spawn points, and goals. This procedural approach simplifies 
level design and allows the mathematical physics bounds to be 
generated simultaneously with the visual models.&]
[s22;:2`.3: 2.3. Continuous State`-Driven Input&]
[s5; Standard operating system keyboard events are designed for typing 
and suffer from repeat`-rate delays, making them unsuitable for 
smooth physical control. A robust input system tracks the boolean 
state of physical keys, e.g. registering when a key goes down 
and when it comes up, allowing the physics simulation to continuously 
poll these states and apply smooth, uninterrupted forces (like 
tilting a board) over time.&]
[s3;:3: 3. Assembling the Demo&]
